{0 Making a game on CamlCast}

From an empty directory to a game you can walk around in, one feature at a time.

A game on this engine {e describes} its world: it says what the world should be
right now, every frame, from nothing, and the runtime works out what changed.
That is React's idea over a raycaster, and it means the two things a game
usually has to invent for itself — somewhere to keep state and a way to get it
onto the screen — are already here.

Every program below compiles. [examples/described_room.ml] and
[examples/described_fuse.ml] are built with the rest of this repository so they
cannot drift from the engine, and every demo under [demo/] is a worked example
of one step.

{1 Four things to know before the first line}

{2 There is no level file format}

A level is OCaml. There is no editor, no [.map] to parse, and nothing to load at
start-up. A room is a value, a world is a value, and a level that varies with
the time of day is a function.

{2 The engine holds no content}

Not one colour, pattern, picture or room. What it has instead are the types
those things are values of, so a game supplies its own and two games can share
an engine without sharing a look.

{2 One library}

{[
  open Camlcast
]}

That is the whole of it. What is in {!Camlcast} is what a description is made
of; what is not is the platform underneath — [Engine], [Renderer],
[Framebuffer], [World], [Player]. Those are reachable by adding [camlcast.core]
to your dune file and saying so, which makes reaching past the layer a decision
with a diff rather than something autocomplete finds for you. You will not need
it.

{2 Three ways of being wrong}

A mistake in a level — a wall of no length, a doorway wider than the wall it is
cut into — raises [Invalid_argument] where you wrote it. Those are bugs in your
program, not conditions it can be in.

Something the world outside would not do — SDL refusing a window, a missing
picture — comes back as [Error (`Msg _)]. Those are conditions.

And the third, which is what {!Camlcast.Check} is for: a level that builds
perfectly and is still wrong. A room nothing leads to, a spawn inside a wall, a
step in the floor at a doorway. Step 14.

{1 Step 0 — A project that depends on the engine}

The engine is not on opam yet, so pin it from git.

{@sh[
mkdir mygame && cd mygame
opam switch create . 5.5.0                # or use a switch you already have
eval $(opam env --switch=. --set-switch)  # so the pin lands in it, whatever
                                          # OPAMSWITCH was pointing at before
opam pin add camlcast git+https://github.com/pharick/camlcast.git
]}

{b If the pin fails on macOS}, it is opam's depext check: Homebrew ships
[sdl2-compat] under the name [sdl2] and opam cannot see it. Install the system
libraries yourself and tell opam to stop looking:

{@sh[
brew install sdl2 sdl2_image
opam pin add camlcast git+https://github.com/pharick/camlcast.git --no-depexts
]}

On Debian or Ubuntu, opam resolves [libsdl2-dev] and [libsdl2-image-dev] through
apt without help. On Windows, use the MSYS2 environment.

Three files. [dune-project]:

{[
(lang dune 3.21)
(name mygame)
]}

[bin/dune]:

{[
(executable
 (name main)
 (libraries camlcast))
]}

And [bin/main.ml], which is Step 1.

{1 Step 1 — One room}

{[
  open Camlcast

  let stone =
    Material.make
      ~pattern:
        (Texture.generate (fun ~u ~v ->
             Color.level (Color.rgb 150 150 160)
               (if ((u / 16) + (v / 16)) land 1 = 0 then 240 else 170)))

  let ground = Plane.horizontal 0.
  let height = 4.

  let level =
    P.(
      world ~atmosphere:Atmosphere.default ~spawn:("room", Vec.make (-4.5) 0.)
        [
          room ~name:"room"
            ~floor:(floor ~plane:ground ~material:stone)
            ~ceiling:(roof ~plane:(Plane.above ground height) ~material:stone)
            [
              outline ~height ~material:stone
                [
                  Vec.make (-6.) (-6.);
                  Vec.make 6. (-6.);
                  Vec.make 6. 6.;
                  Vec.make (-6.) 6.;
                ];
            ];
        ])

  let () =
    match Run.play ~title:"My game" level with
    | Ok _ending -> ()
    | Error (`Msg m) ->
        prerr_endline m;
        exit 1
]}

[dune exec bin/main.exe] and walk about with WASD and the mouse. Escape leaves.

{2 What each piece is}

{!Camlcast.P} holds the parts a world is made of, and is written [P.( ... )]
around a description rather than opened over the whole file. Its names are short
and ordinary — [wall], [room], [text], [path] — so a local open puts them
exactly where a world is being written and nowhere else, and marks where that
starts and stops.

Distances are in {b cells}. One cell is one texture repeat, and the eye stands
half a cell up, so a twelve-cell room under a four-cell ceiling reads as a hall.

{!Camlcast.P.outline} is a closed boundary through the corners you give it.
{b The order does not matter}: it measures the loop and winds it so the room is
on the inside. A boundary wound the wrong way round draws a room black from
within, it is never what anybody wanted, and this is why you will not meet it.

{1 Step 2 — More than one material}

[outline] gives every side the same material. Name the corners and write the
walls yourself when they should differ:

{[
  P.
    [
      wall ~height ~material:brick sw se;
      wall ~height ~material:panel se ne;
      wall ~height ~material:stone ne nw;
      wall ~height ~material:tile nw sw;
    ]
]}

A pattern is a pure function from a texel coordinate to a colour, and
{!Camlcast.Texture.generate} turns one into a tiling square.
{!Camlcast.Texture.generate_masked} returns a colour {e and} an alpha, which is
how a grille or a leaded window is made — see [demo/glass.ml].
{!Camlcast.Texture.of_asset} reads a PNG instead; see [demo/loading.ml].

[demo/masonry.ml] is this step.

{1 Step 3 — The air}

One {!Camlcast.Atmosphere} per world: how far you can see, what colour the
distance is, and where the light comes from.

{[
  let air =
    Atmosphere.make ~haze:(Color.rgb 38 44 58) ~fog_distance:13.
      ~min_brightness:0.15 ~light:(Vec.make (-0.5) (-0.85)) ~ambient:0.35
      ~directional:0.65 ()
]}

[directional] is how much of a wall's shading depends on which way it faces and
[ambient] is how much does not, so a six-sided pillar under a high [directional]
reads as six different greys. [demo/haze.ml] is a colonnade built to show it.

{1 Step 4 — Things in the room}

A {b decal} is a picture fixed flat on a wall, placed in that wall's own terms:
[along] from the end it starts at, [z] up from the floor.

{[
  P.wall ~height ~material:brick a b
    ~decals:
      [ P.decal ~along:2. ~z:1.6 ~half_width:0.9 ~half_height:0.9 painting ]
]}

[~facing:Back] hangs it on the far face, so walking round the wall is what finds
it, and [~glow] lifts it clear of the light the rest of the wall is under — a
sign that is its own light source.

A {b sprite} stands in the room and turns to face you. It is sized by its height
and takes its width from the picture; [~base] floats it clear of the floor.

{[
  P.sprite ~key:"barrel" ~size:0.9 ~image:barrel (Vec.make 2. (-1.))
]}

{b Key anything that can be rearranged.} A list of sprites that sorts itself by
distance keeps each sprite's own state only if the sprites are keyed — see Step 7
for what state that is.

[demo/gallery.ml] and [demo/floating.ml] are this step.

{1 Step 5 — A second room}

Two rooms are joined at a {b doorway} they both describe and a {b link} that
says the two are the same one.

{[
  P.(
    world ~atmosphere:air ~spawn:("west", Vec.make (-3.) 0.)
      [
        room ~name:"west" ~floor:west_floor ~ceiling:west_roof
          [
            path ~height ~material:stone [ a; b; c; d ];
            doorway ~name:"east" ~width:2. ~opening:2.5 ~height
              ~material:stone d a;
          ];
        room ~name:"east" ~floor:east_floor ~ceiling:east_roof [ (* ... *) ];
        link ("west", "east") ("east", "west");
      ])
]}

{b Three sides run as a {!Camlcast.P.path} and the fourth is cut.} That is the
shape of a boundary with a hole in it. An [outline] of all four corners {e and}
a doorway along one of them is a solid wall standing behind an opening — a
doorway you can see through and cannot walk through. [path] winds itself by
measuring the loop it would be if its ends were joined, so it asks no more of you
than [outline] does.

Every room has its own coordinates and there is no world-wide compass. Which
means a neighbour's floor has to be {e carried} across the doorway rather than
written out again:

{[
  let gate = P.opening ~width:2. d a
  and back = P.opening ~width:2. a' d' in
  let east_floor = P.through ~from:gate ~into:back west_floor
]}

A second floor written by hand to look right is one that will drift. Derived, it
cannot — and {!Camlcast.Check} reports a step in the floor at a doorway, so you
would hear about it either way. [demo/slopes.ml] and [demo/portals.ml] are this
step.

{1 Step 6 — Your first component}

Everything so far has been one value. A component is a function from props to a
description, and it is what lets a part of a world keep something of its own.

{[
  let torch =
    Element.declare ~name:"torch" @@ fun pos -> P.sprite ~size:0.8 ~image:flame pos
]}

Use it wherever a part of a world goes:

{[
  P.room ~name:"hall" ~floor ~ceiling
    [ outline ~height ~material:stone corners; torch here; torch there ]
]}

{b Declare components at the top level.} This is a rule and not a style. A
component is identified by its function being physically the same closure,
because a function is the only thing a component is — so one built inside another
render is a fresh closure every frame, is never the same component as last frame,
and is torn down and rebuilt along with everything under it. State will appear
not to work. {!Camlcast.Element.declare} exists partly to make the right thing
the easy one.

{1 Step 7 — State}

{[
  let torch =
    Element.declare ~name:"torch" @@ fun pos ->
    let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state true in
    P.sprite ~size:0.8 ~image:(if lit then flame else stub) pos
]}

[use_state] gives back what this torch is holding and a way to replace it. Every
[torch] in the world has its own, and none of them is written down anywhere else.

Two rules come with it, and both are short.

{b Call hooks unconditionally, in the same order, every render.} Not inside an
[if], not inside a loop whose length varies. Nothing names a hook, so its
position is its identity. Break the rule and you get
{!Camlcast.Hook.Hook_order_changed} rather than a value that was never there.

{b A setter shows up next frame.} It writes its slot and marks the tree; it does
not re-enter the render that is running. At sixty frames a second that is sixteen
milliseconds, and in exchange no render can fail to settle.

{!Camlcast.Hook} also has [use_ref], for what a component needs to remember but
the picture does not depend on, and [use_memo] for what is expensive to work out.

{1 Step 8 — Time}

{[
  let fuse =
    Element.declare ~name:"fuse" @@ fun () ->
    let left, set_left = Hook.use_state 12. in
    Events.use_frame (fun ~dt -> set_left (Float.max 0. (left -. dt)));
    P.world ~atmosphere:(air ~light:(left /. 12.)) ~spawn [ (* ... *) ]
]}

{!Camlcast.Events.use_frame} runs after every frame, with how long it lasted.
Anything reached from there may set state.

It runs {e after} the frame and never during a render, because a render is meant
to be a pure function of props and state — a handler that set state in the middle
of one would make what a frame shows depend on the order its components happened
to be walked in.

{1 Step 9 — Input}

{[
  Events.use_key_down Key.space (fun () -> set_burning true);
  let running = Events.use_key_held Key.lshift in
]}

[use_key_down] is the tap and [use_key_held] the hold.
{!Camlcast.Events.use_actions} is everything else: how long a key has been held,
where the pointer is, which mouse buttons are down.

{b Walking is written with none of them.} That is the bindings' job, and it
reaches your description as movement without a component asking. To change which
keys walk, hand {!Camlcast.Run.play} a {!Camlcast.Binding.t} of your own —
[demo/controls.ml] adds a second set beside WASD in about a dozen lines.

{1 Step 10 — Being looked at}

{!Camlcast.P.wall}, {!Camlcast.P.sprite} and {!Camlcast.P.doorway} each take
[on_gaze] and [on_use], because those are the three things an eye can stop on.

{[
  P.doorway ~name:"cellar"
    ?door:(if shut then Some oak else None)
    ~on_gaze:(fun here -> set_prompt (if here then Some "open" else None))
    ~on_use:(fun _ -> set_shut (not shut))
    ~width:1.6 ~opening:2.2 ~height ~material:brick a b
]}

[on_gaze] is an {e enter} and a {e leave} — [true] when the crosshair arrives and
[false] when it goes, and nothing in between. It is not a poll.

[on_use] fires when the player works the use control while looking at it, and is
handed an {!Camlcast.Aim.spot}: how far away, how many doorways through, and for
a wall how far along it and how high up. That last part is what [demo/chalk.ml]
marks a wall with.

{!Camlcast.Events.use_aim} is the same question from the other side, for a
description that wants to say something about whatever is being looked at
without the thing itself having to say so.

{1 Step 11 — Drawing over the world}

{[
  P.hud
    [
      P.bar ~x:10 ~y:10 ~w:120 ~h:8 ~fraction:(left /. 12.)
        ~color:(Color.rgb 230 190 90) ();
      P.text ~font ~x:10 ~y:24 "press E";
      P.crosshair ();
    ]
]}

A child of [world], drawn over the finished frame in the order it is written.
{!Camlcast.P.highlight} rings whatever the crosshair is on — a rectangle for a
sprite, and for a picture on a wall the trapezoid its four corners project to,
because a wall recedes.

Coordinates are the {b buffer's}, not the window's. The engine renders at
whatever whole-number fraction of the window keeps it under
[Config.max_render_height] and stretches the result, so a thousand-pixel window
is commonly a five-hundred-pixel buffer. {!Camlcast.Events.use_viewport} is how a
component asks how big it actually is.

The engine holds no font, so bring one: {!Camlcast.Font.make} over an
{!Camlcast.Image} atlas. [demo/text.ml] is a page of wrapping, measuring and
clipping.

{1 Step 12 — Composing}

A higher-order component takes children and puts something around them. There is
no machinery to it, because a description is data and a component is a function:

{[
  type lit = { light : float; spawn : string * Vec.t; children : P.t list }

  let lit_world =
    Element.declare ~name:"lit_world" @@ fun props ->
    P.world ~atmosphere:(air ~light:props.light) ~spawn:props.spawn
      props.children
]}

This is the whole point of the layer. [examples/described_fuse.ml] is a small
game built out of two of them, and reading it beside [examples/game.ml] — the
same game written against the platform directly — is the shortest account of what
changed. There, one record holds a phase, a clock and a player, advanced by one
[update], because that is the only shape the older API has. Here the phase and
the clock belong to the component that uses them, the player belongs to the
runtime, and there is nowhere all three are written down together.

{1 Step 13 — State a whole game shares}

Component state belongs to a component. A score, an inventory, which chapter the
player is in — that belongs to nobody in particular, and threading it down as
props is how a codebase acquires arguments that exist only to be passed on.

{[
  type action = Scored of int | Took of string

  let game =
    Store.create
      ~initial:{ score = 0; carrying = [] }
      ~reducer:(fun state -> function
        | Scored n -> { state with score = state.score + n }
        | Took what -> { state with carrying = what :: state.carrying })

  (* and, in a component *)
  let score = Store.use_selector game (fun s -> s.score) in
  ...
  ~on_use:(fun _ -> Store.dispatch game (Scored 10))
]}

Select the smallest thing that will do: a component reading [fun s -> s.score] is
untouched by an action that only moves the player.

{1 Step 14 — What is wrong with your level}

{[
  let () =
    match Check.report level with
    | [] -> ()
    | found -> prerr_endline (Check.format found)
]}

It reads a description for what a compiler cannot see: two doorways joined to the
same third, a room nothing leads to, a spawn inside a wall, a doorway whose ends
meet no wall, a step in the floor where two rooms meet.

It is a pure function of a description, so its natural home is a test — one case
per level, and your levels are covered by [dune runtest] for the cost of writing
that down. Where it can, a diagnostic names the {e component} that wrote the
offending part rather than the room the engine found it in.

{1 Step 15 — The map}

Press {b F3} in a running game and the room is drawn from above: every wall with
a short tick pointing the way its normal faces, doorways green when linked and
red when they lead nowhere, the player's disc with its collision ring, and a mark
on anything {!Camlcast.Check} has something to say about.

The ticks are the useful part. A boundary wound the wrong way round is a row of
them pointing out of the room — the fastest way to see a mistake that otherwise
shows up only as a room that is black from inside.

[Run.play ~debug:false] takes the key away for a game that has stopped wanting
it.

{1 Step 16 — Endings, and the camera}

A description says it is over the same way it says everything else:

{[
  if won then P.finish else Element.empty
]}

The frame it appears in is drawn, and then the run stops.

{!Camlcast.P.camera} is the other half. Without one, the runtime holds the player
and walks it from the bindings. With one, the description says where the eye is,
every frame, and the controls are not applied — which is what a cutscene, a lift
or a death wants. It is the same controlled-or-not distinction a text input has.
Take it out again and the player carries on from wherever it was last put.

{!Camlcast.P.cursor} frees the mouse for a screen drawn over the world, instead
of capturing it for looking around.

{1 Step 17 — Shipping}

[dune build --profile release]. The binary needs SDL2 and SDL2_image at run time,
and any art you read from disk found relative to it — see {!Camlcast.Asset} for
the search order, and [tools/bundle-*.sh] in this repository for scripts that put
an executable, its libraries and its pictures into one directory per platform.

